                <pb facs="0771" n="677" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg677"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">707.1161/17: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Mexico</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Sheffield</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-11-03T13:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 3,
                                1924—1 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>506. Your 396, October 30, noon. If the new Soviet Minister is a duly accredited
                    diplomatic representative and you are called upon to present him to the
                    President at the official reception on December 1, you should do so. If you are
                    obliged, as Dean, to call meeting of the Diplomatic Corps, Soviet representative
                    should be notified, if present in the capital.</p>
                <p>Should you have to see Soviet Minister at official affairs, the fact that we have
                    not recognized the Moscow regime should cause you no embarrassment. Unpleasant
                    incidents can be avoided by assuming a dignified and formal attitude.</p>
                <p>If Soviet representative calls, you may informally receive him but you should not
                    return his call.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
